According to recent discussions in this group there are several
Nethackers who think that the power of a wand of polymorph should
be reduced somehow.  I'm not sure I agree with that, but I do think
that it shouldn't be so easy to get one.  All you have to do is to
look for a wizard that starts out with one.  After you do this a couple
of times you can play another character type and find one of the wands
in a ghost level.  In my best games I used the power of this wand to
create hundreds of wishes (the development team has made this harder
to do, but it's still possible).

I think that a wizard should never start a game with a wand of
polymorph.  It is too tempting to quit any wizard that doesn't
get one at the start of the game.  Players should be encouraged
to play with whatever equipment their wizard starts with, and they
should be discouraged from looking for just the right starting point
in order to obtain such a large advantage.  (Starting with a wand of
polymorph virtually ensures getting good equipment later in the game.)

On the other hand, starting with a wand of polymorph does lead to some
very interesting strategies for an experienced player.  To encourage
creative play that is characterized by even fewer "brute force"
techniques than are required now, I propose a new character class:

A character that is a wimpy wizard with a wand of polymorph.  He starts
with
	+0 cloak of magic resistance
	+0 athame
	blindfold
	3 food rations
	wand of polymorph (4)   (Small number of charges)
	clove of garlic

His attributes are the same as for a wizard, except that he has even
less strength and dexterity.  He might already be telepathic.

In order to survive, he must go slowly thru the first few levels using
the best of fighting techniques.  He must collect as much stuff as he
can, until he meets up with the rothes or ants, and polymorph the pile
to get better equipment so that he can survive these tougher monsters
which usually start appearing on dlevel 4 or 5.

Before I describe some techniques that might be sufficient to keep this
kind of character alive let's consider one thing.  If this character dies
he might leave a wand of polymorph on a bones level for some stronger
character to pick up later.  This would make it easy to set up some ghost
levels with wands of polymorph that could give that next character a strong
advantage.  This new character class should never leave behind a ghost
level when he dies.  Either that or the wands of polymorph should be
removed from the level by the program before the bones file is saved.

The techniques needed for this character are spoilers for the game.



He gets an athame because it's so good for enscribing.

His blindfold is so that he can get telepathy and be able to anticipate
who he has to fight.  Telepathy is very useful because you
can shut doors and keep certain types of monsters from attacking
you when you're not ready for them.  Also, if the orcs or ants are
coming you can make a break for the stairs or for another good place
to fight.

The food rations allow him to go more slowly on the lower levels
than other characters.  In addition, he can use prayer to increase
his hit points rather than to take care of his hunger.  (Stand on a
staircase and let a rat or jackal bite you until you have ~5 HP's.
Then go down the stairs and kick a wall until you have <5 HP's.  If
it's time to pray, then the gods usually give you 5 extra HP's.)

Finally, with his wand of polymorph he can gather all of the weapons he
finds and polymorph them hoping for a long sword.  He can then dip it
into a fountain to get Excalibur.  With this sword it is relatively
easy to survive several more levels of the dungeon.  (This makes
the valkyrie one of the most playable character classes.)

In addition to the inventory above, he perhaps should be a lawful
chartacter so that he can more easily create holy water and work
even more magic.

The strategy for this character might be to collect as many weapons
as possible, maybe even risking fighting some orcs to get more, and
polymorphing to get a long sword.  The fewer charges he uses for this
the more he'll have to polymorph tools to get magic lamps and other
useful tools.  With his holy water he can bless a magic lamp and get a
wish.  (What would he wish for?  Another wand of polymorph, of course!)
He makes a choice on how long he waits to polymorph his weapons to
ensure getting a long sword on the first try in order to save charges
for later.

The point of introducing this character is to encourage the use of
magic in order to survive even the lower levels.  With this change
a player can start out a game with a wand of polymorph, but he has
to play very well to survive.  He probably won't survive unless he
uses the wand to turn some magic at the earliest moment.  Much strategy
would be required to save some charges on the wand for later use in the
hope of creating wishes and for overcoming his weaknesses.


I congratulate the development team for creating a game where cunning
wins out over brutishness.  Now that I'm getting used to NH3.0, I'm
very impressed with all aspects of this version, including those nasty
doors and tough monsters.  I think that this new character would bring
the game even closer to their goals.


